{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And Jacob kissed Rachel.</b> She was young, not yet fit for marriage, and therefore Laban entrusted the flock in her hand and did not entrust them in the hand of Leah, who was older than she, for Leah was fit for a man and stayed in the tent. Or we may say that this kiss was upon the head or the shoulder, for Scripture does not say 'on the mouth' regarding Rachel." "<b>And he lifted up his voice and wept.</b> The custom of relatives upon meeting. And the opinion of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, is that he foresaw through the holy spirit that she would not enter the grave with him (i.e., be buried alongside him)." "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> Why did he weep? Jacob said: Eliezer, my father's servant, brought in his hand nose-rings and bracelets, and I have nothing in my hand, because Eliphaz son of Esau pursued him at his father's command to kill him and overtook him; and because Eliphaz son of Esau had grown up in Isaac's lap, he stayed his hand. He said to him: What shall I do about my father's command? Jacob said to him: Take what is in my hand, for the poor man is considered as dead."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:08.839655634Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}